§ `The spouse's service: (d)(9)(A), (d)(12)(A) and (b)(5)(C)(ii)(II)`

-- 20 (d)(9)(A), line 108: the running of the 5-year period is suspended "during any period that such individual or such individual's spouse is serving on qualified official extended duty" (line 146 says the same for the Peace Corps).
-- So the service of the electing individual's spouse suspends the electing individual's own 5-year period, and the election is the electing individual's own.
-- Sale on 15 March 2022. The taxpayer owned the home from 1 January 2012 to the sale and lived in it from 1 January 2012 to 31 December 2013 only (731 days: 2012 is a leap year).
-- The spouse served from 1 January 2014 to 31 December 2021 on duty 60 miles from the home, under a call of 200 days.
-- Without the spouse's service the 5-year period is 16 March 2017 to 15 March 2022 and holds no day of use: (a) fails, and with no reason for the sale so does (c): $0.
-- With it: walk back from the sale counting the days that are not suspended until 1826 are reached (the days of 16 March 2017 to 15 March 2022: 5 x 365 plus 29 February 2020).
-- 1 January to 15 March 2022 is 31 + 28 + 15 = 74 days; 2013 adds 365 (439); 2012 adds 366 (805); 2011 and 2010 add 365 each (1535); 291 more come from 2009, so the period begins on the 291st day back from 31 December 2009, which is 16 March 2009.
-- The extension is 8 years, inside the cap of 10. The use of 731 days is in the period, and 2 years ending on the sale is 730 days (16 March 2020 to 15 March 2022): (a) is met on the taxpayer's own record.
